speechtext:
I move,
Mr. Chairman, that section 22 be amended as follows:
1.	Page 9, lines 35 and 36: Strike out the words "relating to gold or silver mining" and substitute therefor "relating to the mining of gold, silver, platinum or other precious metals";
2.	Page 9, line 39: After the word "silver" insert "platinum or other precious metals";
3.	Page 9, line 42: Strike out the words "gold or silver" and substitute therefor "gold, silver, platinum or other precious metals";
4.	Page 9, line 44: Strike out the words "gold or silver" and substitute therefor "gold, silver, platinum or other precious metals";
5. Page 9, line 47: Strike out the words "gold or silver" and substitute therefor "gold, silver, platinum or other precious metals";
6. Page 10, line 8: Strike out the words "gold or silver" and substitute therefor "gold, silver, platinum or other precious metals";
7.	Page 10, line 10: Strike out the words "gold or silver" and substitute therefor "gold, silver, platinum or other precious metals";
8.	Page 11, lines 11 and 12: Strike out the words "gold or silver" and substitute therefor "gold, silver, platinum or other precious metals."

We are paying three per cent to this bank every year as interest on our bonds, and what we get back is that proportion of the money to which we are entitled. Why should we pay interest on bonds that were put up originally for a purpose which is now discharged by the country as a whole? Section 41, which deals with that phase of the matter, reads:
No statute relating to the insolvency or winding up of any corporation shall apply to the bank and in no case shall the affairs of the bank be wound up unless parliament so provides;
In other words, there is the recognition that this is an arm of government. It is no longer subject to the normal laws governing corporations that do business of this kind. We pay an interest rate of three per cent, which amounts to $4,500,000. We might just as well cut that payment right off.
